A||||18||ccollins||What tire sizes do you use on the standard 15" wheels?||||1||
Z||000000||ccollins||01-18-2001||11:21 AM||||I'm looking to buy some new tires and was curious what size(s) you all have used and recommend.  <P>I noticed that the front is a 205-60-15 and a 215-60-15 on the rear.  Is it recommended that they be different?  I really would like to use the same size for rotation if possible. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||01-18-2001||11:47 AM||||Mine's lowered, and I run 225/60/15 in back with 215/60/15 in front, to get a slightly taller and wider tire that fills the wheel well better.  A friend has a stock height SE with 225/60/15 all around on GT wheels, but he gets a lot of road spray from the fronts.  I think the diameter difference is roughly 1" from 205 to 225, but that will vary slightly depending on the tire.<P>I don't worry about rotation, since my last set wore pretty evenly with the poly bushings||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||01-18-2001||12:24 PM||||I know that 88 Formulas and GTs have those sizes as standard ( rims are 15x6 front 15x7 rear), not sure on other models/years.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||01-18-2001||12:29 PM||||On my lowered 86 SE with GT style wheels (15") I running 225/50HR15's.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||01-18-2001||02:07 PM||||My winter tires for my 88 Formula are 235/60/14 on Fiero 14" rims. The regular tires are 205/60/15 front and 215/60/15 rear. The front rims are narrower on 88s so I can't rotate anyway.<P>Wider front tires do kick up more road spray. Another disanvantage is they seem to make it want to wander more (follow the grade or grooves in the road).||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||01-18-2001||02:15 PM||||I run 235/60-15's front and 245/60-15's rear on '87 GT wheels. I am using Dunlop GT Qualifiers (cheap) There are quite a few better performing tires out there.<BR>On the rear I have about 5/16" clearance between the tire and the spring perch on the strut.<BR>On the front, the tires rub the inside of the wheel houses slightly during parking lot manuevers.<BR>An '88 should be able to take 245/60-15's front on '87 wheels. The smaller scrub radius of the '88 front suspension will alleviate the rubbing problem.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sluppy123||01-18-2001||05:23 PM||||oops<p>[This message has been edited by sluppy123 (edited 01-18-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000007||sluppy123||01-18-2001||05:23 PM||||225/60ZR15 rear 205/60ZR15 front, Dunlop SP Sport 5000's <BR><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=7653930&p=37756786&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||3
Z||000008||Fformula88||01-18-2001||05:56 PM||||I was running 215/60 R15's on all four corners of my 88 Formula when I first bought it (thats how it was).  When it came time for new tires I went with the recommended 205/60's.  I noticed a very large difference in the steering.  Response was better and turning the wheel was easier.  Neither by a huge margin, but better.  <P>I am running Dunlop D60 A2's.  For the money, I think there about as good of a performance tire there is.  They have a TON of grip!  On my Probe I have Pirreli P4000's.  They are also very nice, but offer better winter traction than the Dunlops.  They grip well too, but its hard to compare with the differences in the cars they are on.  I would recommend either.||||reg||1
Z||000009||gixxer||01-18-2001||06:34 PM||||Yesterday ordered 205/50-15's for the front,<BR>and 225/50-15's Dunlop SP8000s for the rear of my Formula.<BR>I'll let you know if they suck or not.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ccollins||01-18-2001||07:01 PM||||You putting on 50's or 60's? <P>On standard 87 GT wheels will 50's fit? and still have enough clearance?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ray b||01-19-2001||03:18 AM|||| my 87 sev6 had 225-50-15 all round. if do <P>over i would use 195-45-15 frt 245-50-15 rear<P>yoko makes 195-45 new size sb about same tread with as 205-60 plus lowers front 1 1/2<BR>    ray||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||01-19-2001||04:49 AM||||You can use 50 series tires, but if your Fiero isn't lowered it won't look really good.. pretty big wheel gap.<P>If you have the stock 15x7 wheels, you really shouldn't go larger than a 225.  Anything bigger leaves too much of the tire unsupported, and could lead to bad handling.  Right now I have 195/60's on the front and 225/60's on the rear, but I think I'm going to go to 215/60's on the rear.. Yeah, it looks pretty mean with the wide tires on there, but my only hopes of breaking them loose is if it's raining or there's some sand on the pavement   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Oh yeah, all 86-88 GT's came stock with 205/60r15's front, and 215/60r15's rear.  86-87 had a 15x7 wheel f&r, while 88's got a 15x6 up front with a different offset and the same 15x7 rear.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||
Z||000013||v8vinnie||01-19-2001||07:23 PM||||LOOKS LIKE IM GOING TO HAVE TO USE THE STOCK RIMS AND TIRE SIZES FOR A WHILE SINCE I CANT AFFORD TO GET MY 17INCH RIMS,BUT I REALLY WANT TO PUT Z RATED TIRES ON IN THE STOCK SIZES. DOES ONLY DUNLOP MAKE THE Z RATED TIRES IN STOCK SIZES OR DOES ANY BODY ELSE, I KNOW MY V8 WITH 345 HORSES WILL DO MORE THAN 150MPH, AND TRUST ME I WILL FIND OUT ONCE I GET ALL MY SYSPENSION AND TIRES DONE,LIKE I SAID BEFORE, (i love a good top end run).||||reg||1
Z||000014||danny boy||01-20-2001||12:30 AM||||Top 150mph in a Fiero?Are you nuts? At  120+<BR>in my GT I start worrying.But now you're talkin 'bout it,BF Goodrich makes Zrated stock size tires.I got'em on mine and like them real well.I have Dunlops on my Stealth, 245/45-17,they're Z rated and so far they have held 175...  ||||reg||7
Z||000015||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-20-2001||03:06 AM||||I have an 84 sc with the 88 Formula wheels.<P>I have Kelly HR "Radial GT" 205/60 R 15s all the way around.  The car has amazing grip compared to the 185/80 R 13s it had to begin with.<P>My goal was grip and smooth ride.  50 series tires can punish the driver and car.  They are not what I was looking for.  <P>The Formula wheels look really great on my white 2m4 Fiero.  And, I get 4 wheel tire rotations for free (part of the tire deal).  I bought the wheels at the Fieroshop.  It was a good deal and they are very good condition.||||reg||10
Z||000016||Standard||01-20-2001||04:53 AM||||Are you sure they're 88's?  Like I said above, the 88 lace rims were 15x6 on the front, with a different offset (not sure how much different, anybody know?).  If you have the 15x6 on the front and the 15x7 on the rear, rotating them wouldn't be a good idea, even with the same tire sizes on both ends.<P>If I'm wrong and you have 15x7's on both ends, disregard the above  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-20-2001||05:08 AM||||To rotate my tires, they must be re-mounted.  The off sets and widths are different between the front and rear Formula wheels.  By using all of the same size tires, then it is possible to rotate.  <P>Also, the fiero understeers more with wider tires on the back vs. front.  Having them all the same size may not be the right choice for a V8 or V6 Fiero, but for my 4 cylinder, 205s are plenty of tire to get the power to the ground.  For these reasons, it just seemed logical to use 205s all around on my car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||v8vinnie||01-20-2001||10:39 AM||||thanks, I think ill make a few phone calls to discount tires and such, and find out more info on those tires, oh yea when i had my 1997 c5 corvette i had a 400hp mongoose kit in it and i knuged 180mph on route75, gets pretty scary up there, but im not what they call a bench racer, as long as the road is smooth and no one is around ill take the fiero up ther sooner or later? just gotsta know!!!!||||reg||1
